  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 301 AM EDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nToday...\n\nSurface high pressure across the region will provide quiet weather \nconditions. Light winds combined with damp soils from recent \nrainfall has led to fog development in some areas this morning. \nLocally dense fog cannot be completely ruled out near valleys or \nrivers through daybreak. Look for fog to quickly mix out in typical \ndiurnal fashion after daybreak. Partly cloudy skies are then \nexpected during the day under large scale subsidence. Weak cold air \nadvection behind a cold front which moved through on Monday supports \nslightly cooler than normal highs in the upper 70s to low 80s today.\n\nTonight through Wednesday night...\n\nEmbedded shortwaves within longwave troughing aloft will provide \nopportunities for convection towards the middle of this week. Low \nrain chances return as early as tonight with an approaching \ndisturbance. Most guidance generally suggest lingering subsidence \ninduced dry air from the departing surface high late today greatly \nlimits the potential for precipitation. This along with marginal \nforcing in place keeps POPs around 20 percent or lower through the \novernight. Expect greater rain chances during the day Wednesday once \nstronger forcing and deeper moisture returns. Model variability \nleads to some uncertainty regarding convective coverage. However, \ndaytime heating and an embedded shortwave moving across an \nabundantly moist environment favors at least scattered convection by \nthe afternoon.\n\nSevere weather is not expected due to limited instability. Steep low-\nlevel lapse rates and DCAPE values approaching 600 to 700 J/KG could \nstill result in a few strong sub-severe wind gusts. An isolated \ndamaging wind gust cannot be entirely ruled out if sufficient \ndestabilization occurs. The greater concern appears to be localized \nflash flooding given soils remain saturated from recent rainfall. \nModel guidance also suggest the potential for training storms as a \nweak west to east oriented cold front slowly pushes south late in \nthe day. The weak surface boundary meandering and another embedded \nshortwave moving through keeps precipitation chances in the forecast \novernight. Loss of daytime heating should result in less convective \ncoverage though. \n\nThursday through early next week...\n\nA weak frontal boundary meandering near south-central Indiana on \nThursday will keep low precipitation chances in the forecast. Further \nnorth, surface high pressure begins to build in providing mostly \nquiet weather conditions. Look for the surface high to build further \nsouth Thursday night into Friday resulting in quiet weather \nconditions. Another embedded disturbance moves in on Saturday \nsupporting at least low rain chances with sufficient moisture \nadvecting into central Indiana. Long range guidance generally \ndepicts surface high pressure building in once again late weekend \ninto early next week. Large scale subsidence supports mundane \nweather conditions.",
